The vendor’s words hang in the air—kind, wary, offering a lifeline.

You kneel beside the fallen stall, fingers brushing spilled fruit, cracked pottery—and feel it again: the *thrum* of the Knight of Wands, restless, eager to move, to *ignite*, while the Hermit inside you pulls you back into stillness, into *observation*. The Eight of Cups sighs softly—*you’ve walked away from things like this before. Why not again?*

But your hand hovers above a shattered ceramic bowl, glowing faintly gold.

And then—you *breathe*.

Not a shout. Not a spell.

Just breath.

The shards tremble. Then lift—one by one—as if caught in a gentle tide. They rise, floating like leaves on a quiet stream, drifting back into place. The stall mends itself, whole and warm, as if it had never broken at all.

The vendor blinks.

"...Well,” they murmur, voice softer now. “That wasn’t an accident.”

They meet your gaze. No fear. Only recognition.

“The Guild’s meeting tonight. At the old clocktower. Third floor. Say ‘I hear the bell three times.” 

They toss you a single copper coin—warm to the touch.

“And don’t be late,” they add, turning away—but not before you catch the smallest smile.

Behind you, the bell rings again.

One time.

Then another.

And then—

A third.

It’s calling you home.

